faqs:
  - question: "What is an email trigger in Appwrite Functions?"
    answer: "An email trigger executes an Appwrite Function when an email arrives at an address that is connected to the function. Appwrite receives the email, stores its content and attachments, and sends the parsed email to the function as JSON."
  - question: "Do I need to configure DNS to receive email in a function?"
    answer: "No. Each function has a built-in address that ends with `<FUNCTION_ID>.appwrite.email`, and any name before the `@` works. DNS records are necessary only when you connect your own domain. A custom domain needs an MX record and a TXT record."
  - question: "Can Appwrite Functions send email?"
    answer: "Email triggers only receive email. To send email, use [Appwrite Messaging](/docs/products/messaging) from the function, for example to reply to the sender."
  - question: "What happens when the same email arrives two times?"
    answer: "Appwrite can deliver an email more than one time, for example when the sending mail server retries. Each email has a unique `id`. Use it as the row ID or store it, so that the function processes each email one time."
  - question: "How does a function get the attachments of an email?"
    answer: "The request body contains an `attachments` list. Each attachment has a name, a content type, a size, and a download URL. The URLs expire 5 minutes after the execution starts, so download the files at the start of the function and upload them to Storage if you need them later."
---

Many apps receive work by email. Customers send support requests, vendors send invoices, and monitoring services send alerts. To process that email in code, you usually need an inbound email provider, a webhook endpoint, and a MIME parser.

Appwrite Functions now have **email triggers**. A function can execute when an email arrives at its address. Appwrite receives the email, parses it, stores the attachments, and sends the result to your function as JSON.

# How email triggers work

Each email that arrives at a connected address creates one asynchronous execution of your function. The execution is a `POST` request, and the `x-appwrite-trigger` header is `email`.

Appwrite handles each email in this order:

1. Receive the email over SMTP.
2. Confirm that the address belongs to a function with an active deployment.
3. Store the original email and each attachment.
4. Parse the headers, the plain text body, and the HTML body.
5. Execute the function with the parsed email as the request body.

The function receives JSON, so it needs no MIME parser and no mail server of its own.

# Receive email at the built-in address

{% info title="SMTP-DOCS-TODO: Screenshot pending (blog-built-in-address)" %}
**SMTP-DOCS-TODO screenshot:blog-built-in-address.** Capture the **Domains** tab of a function that shows the built-in email address card. Dark theme, 1460x822 viewport at 2x, screenshot mode on. Save as `/images/blog/announcing-function-email-triggers/built-in-address.avif`, then replace this block with the image.
{% /info %}

Each function has a built-in email address that ends with `<FUNCTION_ID>.appwrite.email`. You can use any name before the `@`, so `support@<FUNCTION_ID>.appwrite.email` and `billing@<FUNCTION_ID>.appwrite.email` both execute the same function. The function can read which address received the email.

The built-in address does not need DNS records.

